  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "Given vectors u, v in a complex inner product space E over RCLike scalars, define orthogonal projection orthProj v u = (⟪ v, u ⟫ / ⟪ v, v ⟫) • v and residual orthResidual v u = u - orthProj v u. Show the projection pipeline yields a Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization for 2 and 3 vectors, with the key connection: Cauchy-Schwarz equality holds for (v, u) iff orthResidual v u = 0 iff u is a scalar multiple of v.",
    "plain": "Can the complex Gram-Schmidt process be formalized using the Cauchy-Schwarz equality characterization? Yes — the orthogonal projection u ↦ u - (⟪ v, u ⟫ / ⟪ v, v ⟫) v is exactly the GS step, and CS equality detects when this step is trivial.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "Connects two classical inner product space results (Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization and Cauchy-Schwarz equality)",
      "Demonstrates that CS equality is exactly the residual-vanishing condition",
      "Provides explicit projection pipeline (projCoeff, orthProj, orthResidual) reusable for higher-dimension GS",
      "Builds on the parent OQ-01-OQ-01 (CS equality characterization)"
    ]
  },
